<h2>A short guided breathing exercise</h2>
<p><strong>Cortisol+ has an animated breathing exercise that gives you a structured pause.</strong></p>
<p>It includes on-screen prompts, a cycle counter and a way to end early.</p>
<p>It does not test cortisol before or after the session.</p>

<h2>What's included?</h2>
<p><strong>Three phases that repeat:</strong> breathe in, hold, and breathe out. Each lasts four seconds.</p>
<p>Eight cycles take about 96 seconds. That's the iPhone exercise we reviewed for this guide.</p>
<p>The start screen calls it "4-4-4 box breathing". This version does <strong>not</strong> include a fourth hold after breathing out.</p>

<h3>How to use it</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Open the breathing exercise and tap <strong>Begin</strong>.</li>
  <li>Follow the circle as it grows and shrinks, and the prompts for each phase. Go at a pace that feels comfortable.</li>
  <li>Tap <strong>End Early</strong> if you want to stop. The completion screen lets you close the exercise.</li>
</ol>
<p>This describes the exercise as we reviewed it on September 9, 2026.</p>
<p>We have removed earlier claims on this website about four selectable breathing patterns and automatic before-and-after hormone measurements. The version we reviewed didn't support those claims.</p>

<ZenTip image="/app-ui/zen_new_breathe.webp">
  Sit somewhere you can let your shoulders drop. If a 4-second hold feels too long, <strong>just breathe gently</strong> and follow the circle.
</ZenTip>

<h2>What does a finished session tell you?</h2>
<p><strong>Finishing the timer records that you used the exercise.</strong> That's all.</p>
<p>It doesn't show that your stress, heart rate or cortisol changed by a promised amount.</p>
<p>If you look at a wearable reading afterward, check the time it was recorded. The latest sample may be from before the exercise.</p>
<p>Notice how the exercise feels, without treating a score as proof it worked. There's no set Cortisol+ result every user should reach after one session or several weeks.</p>

